{"cluster_id": "C-2271", "topic": "E5's in-place-materialize fast path was unreachable from production", "target": "architecture/manifests-and-refs", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-054"], "canonical_claim": "E5's in-place-materialize fast path was unreachable from production: `flushRefBatch`'s trial-validation copy `working` stayed alive to function scope, pinning a second shared_ptr reference to the live base across the post-PUT install so `materializeCommitted()` always saw `use_count()==2` and took the O(N) copy path; fixed by releasing `working` before id allocation so the live base is uniquely owned at the install point.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-21-reftablestate-experiments.md#e5-followup-xhigh"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2272", "topic": "A race existed where the relaxed use_count() load", "target": "architecture/manifests-and-refs", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-055"], "canonical_claim": "A race existed where the relaxed `use_count()` load in the in-place-materialize fast path could race a cross-thread shared_ptr release: `trySnapshotPublishOnce`'s `candidate_state` (a shared-base copy) was destroyed at function return outside the lock. Fixed by resetting `candidate_state` under `state_mutex` immediately after `snapshotOf`, covering every exit path.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-21-reftablestate-experiments.md#e5-followup-xhigh"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2273", "topic": "Risk register #5 (Medium, confirmed): moveDirectory's RENAME TABLE", "target": "architecture/manifests-and-refs", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-240"], "canonical_claim": "Risk register #5 (Medium, confirmed): `moveDirectory`'s RENAME TABLE path (`:980-1006`) is non-atomic across namespaces; a mid-loop throw can leave the table split across two namespaces (documented as re-drivable), producing an operator-visible split state until manual re-drive. Suggested action: track as a known limitation, consider a durable move journal pre-release.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L546"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2274", "topic": "Per-table refs use an append-only log plus periodic", "target": "architecture/manifests-and-refs", "member_ids": ["B001-006", "B011-075", "B037-114", "B038-022", "B043-018"], "canonical_claim": "Per-table refs use an append-only log plus periodic snapshot, and CasRefStateMachine computes RefTableState = replay(snapshot, tail) for writers, recovery, GC intake, and fsck.; Ref tables are persisted as an immutable snapshot plus an append-only log of strictly increasing transaction ids, and recovery uses ordered point reads while cleanup deletes only durably observed objects.; A ref table is one mutable naming layer per table namespace `SERVER_ID/TABLE_UUID`, implemented as an append-only transaction log plus periodic snapshots and mutable checkpoints under one namespace LIFE.; A table's durable ref state is `latest table snapshot + table transaction logs after that snapshot`; snapshots are complete table states and each log object is one atomic writer transaction.; Refs use a writer-owned immutable snapshot plus an append-only log with strictly increasing ids, and a per-table `CasSingleWriterSlot` makes intra-server append conflicts structurally impossible.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-snapshot-log", "docs/superpowers/models/README.md#group-ref-table", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L225-L229", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-11-cas-ref-table-snapshot-log-design.md#summary", "src/Disks/DiskObjectStorage/MetadataStorages/ContentAddressed/README.md#L15-L21"]}
